// en common-words lexicon (SSOT) — pool-INDEPENDENT corroboration for the
// person detector's name-like leading-token signal.
//
// A bare-surname candidate's leading token (the given-name slot) is treated as
// "name-like" when its lowercase form (trailing dot stripped) is NOT in this
// set, it is alphabetic, and its length is >= 2. Name-like adds W_NAME_LIKE
// evidence, so a real Given+Surname pair whose given name is outside the SSA
// pool ("Marco Rossi", "Wei Chen") still corroborates, while a place / common-
// word pair ("Central Park", "Lake Park") does not. This removes the SSA
// pool's Anglo bias from the gate WITHOUT reviving the place/noise FPs.
//
// Hand-maintained curated set: the most frequent English words plus place /
// geographic / directional terms that collide with surnames (the FP drivers).
// Regenerate / refresh from a pinned word-frequency source via:
//   python -m argus_redact.specs.gen_en_common_words
// (the committed file is the source of truth; the generator is provenance +
// future refresh, and unions the fetched top-N with the curated supplement.)
//
// ── Design tension: this list does DOUBLE DUTY ──
//
// It is BOTH (1) the place / common-word FP-suppression set (a leading "Central"
// in "Central Park" must not look name-like) AND (2) the name-like NEGATIVE
// filter (any leading token in this set is declared NOT-a-name). A token that is
// genuinely BOTH a real given name AND a common/place word is therefore
// irreducibly ambiguous in this single-list design — including it suppresses a
// real name, omitting it revives a place / prose FP. There is no curation that
// makes both correct at once.
//
// Curation principle (applied consistently):
//   - PREDOMINANTLY a person name -> OMIT (treat as name-like). Season names
//     ("summer", "autumn", "winter", "spring") and predominantly-name month
//     names ("april", "may", "june", "august") are omitted; the rare
//     "Spring Lake"-style place FP (needs a pooled 2nd token) is the accepted
//     residual.
//   - PREDOMINANTLY a common / function / place word -> INCLUDE (suppress).
//     Ultra-high-frequency words that are ALSO occasionally a name ("will",
//     "major", "drew", "art", "guy", "rich", "royal", "river", "lane", "case",
//     "true", "count") stay in: removing them would fire name-like on ordinary
//     prose. The fast-L1 recall miss ("Will Smith" suppressed) is an ACCEPTED
//     trade — L2 NER (ner / auto mode) recovers it.
//   - Curated place / geo / temporal terms (forest, brook, glen, dale, ridge,
//     liberty, victory, march, july, ...) stay in as FP drivers even when they
//     are an occasional name; the rare-name reading is the residual.
//
// FUTURE WORK: split this into TWO lists — a place / function FP-SUPPRESSION set
//   (used only to keep "Central Park" non-name-like) and a strictly
//   NOT-A-NAME set (used only as the name-like negative filter). A token could
//   then suppress a place FP WITHOUT also vetoing a real given name, dissolving
//   the ambiguity above. Until then the principle is: predominantly-name -> omit;
//   predominantly-common-word -> include and accept the L2-recovered recall miss.
